From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000024 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: OMAP4: cpuidle: register steelhead's deep states disabled

This used to truncate the driver to C1 (state_count = 1) because the
C2/C3 MPUSS power transitions trap into the HS secure monitor and the
historical failure was a CPU1 cpuidle panic with SMP online (stock 3.0.8
guarded the same paths with cpuidle44xx.disallow_smp_idle).

Truncating made the deep states unexaminable: with no registered state
there are no residency counters, so whether mpu_pwrdm can reach CSWR or
OSWR at all could not be measured, and the only way to find out was to
build a kernel that entered them unconditionally -- an experiment whose
failure mode is an unbootable box.

Register C2/C3 with CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF instead. The states exist and carry
counters, but sysfs shows disable=1 and the governor never selects them,
so an ordinary boot behaves exactly as C1-only did. One state can then be
armed by hand for a measured run, and if it hangs, a reboot -- not a
reflash -- restores the safe default.

mpu_pwrdm and core_pwrdm have RET:0 OFF:0 on this board after 20 h of
uptime: retention has never been *requested*, not refused. This is what
makes asking possible.

Signed-off-by: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
---
diff --git a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
index df106524d..2f74d78c3 100644
--- a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <linux/cpuidle.h>
 #include <linux/cpu_pm.h>
 #include <linux/export.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/tick.h>
 
 #include <asm/cpuidle.h>
@@ -326,5 +327,32 @@ int __init omap4_idle_init(void)
 	if (!cpu_clkdm[0] || !cpu_clkdm[1])
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * steelhead (HS OMAP4460): C2 and C3 drive MPUSS power transitions that
+	 * trap into the HS secure monitor. The historical failure was a CPU1
+	 * cpuidle panic with SMP online - stock 3.0.8 guarded the same paths
+	 * with its cpuidle44xx.disallow_smp_idle parameter - so for a long time
+	 * this truncated the driver to C1 (state_count = 1) and the deep states
+	 * did not exist at all.
+	 *
+	 * Register them DISABLED instead. CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF leaves a state
+	 * visible in sysfs with disable=1, so the governor never selects it and
+	 * a boot is exactly as safe as C1-only, while
+	 *
+	 *   echo 0 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state1/disable
+	 *
+	 * arms one deliberately for a measured experiment. If that wedges the
+	 * box a plain reboot restores the safe default - nothing has to be
+	 * reflashed to recover, which is precisely what made this untestable
+	 * before. Registering them also makes their residency counters exist,
+	 * so "did mpu_pwrdm ever actually reach CSWR/OSWR" becomes answerable.
+	 */
+	if (of_machine_is_compatible("google,steelhead")) {
+		int i;
+
+		for (i = 1; i < idle_driver->state_count; i++)
+			idle_driver->states[i].flags |= CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF;
+	}
+
 	return cpuidle_register(idle_driver, cpu_online_mask);
 }
